{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 32, :comments ["<b>And you shall not take ransom for one who has fled to his city of refuge.</b> For one who has fled to a city of refuge, having killed unintentionally — he is not released from exile by money, to give ransom <b>to return to dwell in the land</b> before the priest dies (ibid.)." "<b>For one who has fled.</b> Like לַנָּס, as in ״שׁוּבֵי מִלְחָמָה״ (Micah 2) — those who returned from the war, and likewise ״נוּגֵי מִמּוֹעֵד״ (Zephaniah 3), and likewise ״כִּי מֻלִים הָיוּ״ (Joshua 5); just as you say שׁוּב for one who has already returned, and מוּל for one who is already circumcised, so you say לָנוּס for one who has already fled, and call him נוּס, meaning מֻבְרָח. And if you say לָנוּס — 'to flee,' and interpret it: you shall not take ransom for one who must flee, to release him from exile (cf. Sifrei) — I do not know how it can say ״to return to dwell in the land,״ for he has not yet fled — from where would he return?"], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:56.985984201Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}